<h2 class="mt-12">Peak season readiness for customer retention</h2>
<ol>
<li>Halt customer retention edits ahead of the peak window.</li>
<li>For customer retention, trial every journey against real order events.</li>
<li>For customer retention, confirm buyer and return exclusions hold.</li>
<li>Boost customer retention control groups before the surge.</li>
<li>Draft customer retention SMS quiet-hour and cap rules in advance.</li>
<li>Price customer retention peaks against plan ceilings early.</li>
<li>Customer Retention placement watched every peak day.</li>
<li>Bank customer retention rollback plans ahead of sends.</li>
</ol>
<p>One more customer retention operating note: book the first quarterly review before launch, not after the first crisis. Customer Retention teams that record suppression rules, discount caps, and escalation contacts in week one firefight far less later, and new operators inherit a customer retention system instead of folklore.</p>
<p>Finally, review customer retention in writing each quarter: what customer retention work shipped, what was suppressed, what margin the customer retention program kept, which assumptions failed. That customer retention record turns individual judgment into team knowledge and keeps vendor calls evidence-based.</p>
